  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the liquid in a flow battery?",
      "a": "In the most common type, both tanks hold vanadium dissolved in sulphuric acid, with the vanadium in different charge states on each side. Other types use zinc and bromine, or iron salts.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an electrolyte?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens in the cell stack?",
      "a": "The two liquids flow past electrodes on either side of a membrane. Charging and discharging change the charge of the vanadium ions, sending electrons through the outside circuit.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a flow battery make electricity?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the membrane do in a flow battery?",
      "a": "It keeps the two liquids from mixing while letting small ions, such as hydrogen ions, pass through to balance the charges. Without it, the battery would simply discharge itself.",
      "asks": [
        "Why don't the two liquids mix?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are flow batteries good for the grid?",
      "a": "Energy and power can be sized separately: bigger tanks store more hours, and a bigger cell stack gives more power. They last for decades with little wear and their water-based liquids do not burn.",
      "asks": [
        "Why use flow batteries instead of lithium?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why aren't flow batteries used in phones or cars?",
      "a": "The liquids store far less energy for their weight than lithium-ion cells, so a flow battery is large and heavy. That is fine in a building beside a substation, but useless in your pocket.",
      "asks": [
        "Are flow batteries heavy?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In a flow battery, the energy stored depends on the size of the tanks, while the power depends on the size of the cell stack.",
    "Vanadium flow batteries use vanadium on both sides, so liquid crossing the membrane does not permanently spoil the electrolytes."
  ],
